1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:03,935
An AI coding tool can answer a prompt and still miss the project.

2
00:00:03,885 --> 00:00:10,021
It may reread the same files, overlook dependencies, or propose a change without understanding its impact.

3
00:00:10,021 --> 00:00:15,510
CodeAtlas gives your tools a reusable map of the repository, then selects context for the task.

4
00:00:15,510 --> 00:00:19,562
This walkthrough shows what works today and where the beta still needs attention.

5
00:00:21,308 --> 00:00:27,717
CodeAtlas scans source files, hashes them, extracts symbols, builds relationships, and stores the index locally.

6
00:00:27,667 --> 00:00:30,812
Search and context selection are deterministic.

7
00:00:30,812 --> 00:00:34,187
An AI provider is optional for summaries and briefings.

8
00:00:34,187 --> 00:00:38,031
Today the strongest parsing support is TypeScript and JavaScript.

9
00:00:38,031 --> 00:00:44,594
Treat other languages and unresolved import aliases as limitations, rather than assuming full code intelligence.

10
00:00:46,392 --> 00:00:51,477
Use Node twenty two point five or newer because storage uses the built in SQLite module.

11
00:00:51,427 --> 00:00:55,312
Install codeatlas cli, check the version, and read help.

12
00:00:55,312 --> 00:00:57,656
Installation gives you CodeAtlas.

13
00:00:57,656 --> 00:01:01,333
It does not silently install coding agents or toolkit tools.

14
00:01:01,333 --> 00:01:07,375
This video follows the local zero point five point one source; confirm the installed version when following along.

15
00:01:09,142 --> 00:01:12,873
Enter your project and run atlas scan for a metadata overview.

16
00:01:12,823 --> 00:01:18,323
Scan reports files, languages, and framework evidence without building the context database.

17
00:01:18,323 --> 00:01:21,135
Run atlas init to index the project.

18
00:01:21,135 --> 00:01:24,583
Tools none skips the optional post initialization offer.

19
00:01:24,583 --> 00:01:30,990
These commands use the SDK pipeline, and generated project state lives inside the local codeatlas directory.

20
00:01:32,767 --> 00:01:35,196
Atlas build rebuilds context.

21
00:01:35,146 --> 00:01:40,750
After source changes, use atlas update so unchanged files can reuse their stored information.

22
00:01:40,750 --> 00:01:43,458
A no change update avoids reparsing.

23
00:01:43,458 --> 00:01:49,167
Summaries is a separate opt in flag that requests provider backed enrichment; configure a provider first.

24
00:01:49,167 --> 00:01:54,854
Basic indexing and search do not need a provider, and failed summary requests are reported separately.

25
00:01:56,642 --> 00:02:00,477
Search accepts query words and returns ranked indexed results.

26
00:02:00,427 --> 00:02:05,958
Limit controls the result count, type narrows the kind, and no fuzzy disables typo tolerance.

27
00:02:05,958 --> 00:02:08,521
JSON makes results easy to script.

28
00:02:08,521 --> 00:02:13,229
Explain resolves a file, symbol, module, or concept against the index.

29
00:02:13,229 --> 00:02:18,948
AI summaries require the explicit AI flag; ordinary explanations use local evidence.

30
00:02:20,725 --> 00:02:24,342
Atlas ask returns a ranked context slice for a question.

31
00:02:24,292 --> 00:02:27,021
It is not a general chat answer from a model.

32
00:02:27,021 --> 00:02:35,469
Set max tokens to bound the estimated context size, save to persist the slice, and plan to include task classification and a deterministic plan.

33
00:02:35,469 --> 00:02:38,615
Read the evidence before giving it to your coding agent.

34
00:02:40,725 --> 00:02:46,227
Context build assembles a task specific package with selection reasons and an estimated token budget.

35
00:02:46,177 --> 00:02:48,854
Explain shows why items were selected.

36
00:02:48,854 --> 00:02:55,604
Context mode chooses automatic, digest, full, or disabled assembly, with an automatic escalation option as well.

37
00:02:55,604 --> 00:02:59,052
Include instructions and overview flags tune the package.

38
00:02:59,052 --> 00:03:02,479
The AI flag adds an optional provider backed briefing.

39
00:03:04,267 --> 00:03:07,915
Export writes a self contained markdown slice for your agent.

40
00:03:07,865 --> 00:03:11,990
Choose Claude, Gemini, Codex, OpenCode, or generic.

41
00:03:11,990 --> 00:03:15,083
Use no inject when you only want the export file.

42
00:03:15,083 --> 00:03:20,354
Otherwise an agent instruction file can receive a marked block, with a backup on first injection.

43
00:03:20,354 --> 00:03:24,615
Review both the slice and the target instruction file before using the result.

44
00:03:26,392 --> 00:03:31,133
Agents status shows MCP registration status for supported coding tools.

45
00:03:31,083 --> 00:03:37,156
Connect can register the context server for an installed target; dry run previews the configuration changes.

46
00:03:37,156 --> 00:03:42,906
Atlas MCP serves context over standard input and output, so your client normally launches it.

47
00:03:42,906 --> 00:03:48,344
That gives the coding agent access to indexed context tools while preserving the SDK boundary.

48
00:03:50,100 --> 00:03:54,112
The four agent commands wrap context launch with a provider selection.

49
00:03:54,062 --> 00:03:59,427
OpenCode, Claude, Gemini, and Codex must be installed and configured independently.

50
00:03:59,427 --> 00:04:02,771
You can add a skill and an estimated context budget.

51
00:04:02,771 --> 00:04:10,687
The present launch path does not provide a reliable visible transcript for external CLIs, so test the handoff before relying on it in daily work.

52
00:04:12,475 --> 00:04:17,415
Here is a real run of the installed OpenCode CLI on the tiny authentication example.

53
00:04:17,365 --> 00:04:21,729
The configured default provider failed because its account had insufficient funds.

54
00:04:21,729 --> 00:04:24,656
An explicit Big Pickle model run succeeded.

55
00:04:24,656 --> 00:04:28,021
Its actual response explains authenticate and login.

56
00:04:28,021 --> 00:04:35,219
This is a direct OpenCode demonstration; the Atlas launcher still needs visible output handoff before it can provide the same experience.

57
00:04:36,975 --> 00:04:40,269
Sessions exposes list, info, and stop.

58
00:04:40,219 --> 00:04:43,573
However, the current manager keeps sessions in memory.

59
00:04:43,573 --> 00:04:48,990
A separate CLI invocation creates a fresh manager and cannot recover the session you just launched.

60
00:04:48,990 --> 00:04:52,073
Context attach is limited to a created session.

61
00:04:52,073 --> 00:04:58,906
These APIs are useful within a shared SDK manager, but persistent command line session management needs further work.

62
00:05:00,683 --> 00:05:05,050
Providers reports which AI providers are configured without printing keys.

63
00:05:05,000 --> 00:05:11,198
Ollama offers status, connect, models, use, and disconnect for a local or configured remote service.

64
00:05:11,198 --> 00:05:14,052
Pick a model your server actually exposes.

65
00:05:14,052 --> 00:05:17,198
AI summaries and briefings are explicit choices.

66
00:05:17,198 --> 00:05:23,344
Keep keys in user configuration or the environment, and avoid placing real credentials in tutorial commands.

67
00:05:25,100 --> 00:05:29,290
Usage summary, list, and budgets report recorded AI activity.

68
00:05:29,240 --> 00:05:33,802
Values can be actual, estimated, or unknown; unknown is not zero.

69
00:05:33,802 --> 00:05:37,167
External CLI token usage is often unavailable.

70
00:05:37,167 --> 00:05:41,500
Metrics show reports local activity and estimated context compression.

71
00:05:41,500 --> 00:05:45,635
Export writes JSON or CSV, and reset requires yes.

72
00:05:45,635 --> 00:05:50,219
Estimated savings are a heuristic baseline, not a measured performance guarantee.

73
00:05:51,975 --> 00:05:59,998
Tools lists the toolkit overview, categories groups the catalog, search finds entries, and info shows declared metadata and installed state.

74
00:05:59,948 --> 00:06:03,542
Setup detects project evidence and proposes a selection.

75
00:06:03,542 --> 00:06:05,740
Dry run previews the plan.

76
00:06:05,740 --> 00:06:08,802
Nothing should install just because it is recommended.

77
00:06:08,802 --> 00:06:14,917
Review compatibility, trust, the official distribution channel, and the exact command before approving a tool.

78
00:06:16,683 --> 00:06:19,633
Install requires explicit approval with yes.

79
00:06:19,583 --> 00:06:23,708
Configure previews supported host configuration changes with dry run.

80
00:06:23,708 --> 00:06:27,573
Tools doctor checks installed manifests and integration state.

81
00:06:27,573 --> 00:06:33,229
Update can approve an ecosystem refresh, and remove delegates removal to the appropriate ecosystem.

82
00:06:33,229 --> 00:06:39,406
These are real machine changes, so inspect the selected tool and its declared requirements before running the commands.

83
00:06:41,183 --> 00:06:44,685
Custom tools use the existing schema and local overlay.

84
00:06:44,635 --> 00:06:47,906
Validate checks a JSON definition before writing it.

85
00:06:47,906 --> 00:06:53,167
Add persists a valid record, and create builds one from command flags and install metadata.

86
00:06:53,167 --> 00:06:59,510
Read create help for the full required shape, including official package and repository information where applicable.

87
00:06:59,510 --> 00:07:03,135
Replace must be explicit when overriding an existing record.

88
00:07:04,892 --> 00:07:09,112
Skills are reusable instructions in a canonical skill markdown format.

89
00:07:09,062 --> 00:07:11,771
List shows built in and installed skills.

90
00:07:11,771 --> 00:07:17,010
Info inspects metadata, validate checks the definition, and load renders instructions.

91
00:07:17,010 --> 00:07:19,208
The skill alias works too.

92
00:07:19,208 --> 00:07:22,302
Inject a named skill with the skill flag on a launcher.

93
00:07:22,302 --> 00:07:27,625
Project skills resolve before built ins, and unknown skill identifiers fail before launch.

94
00:07:29,392 --> 00:07:32,665
Create generates a validated project skill template.

95
00:07:32,615 --> 00:07:37,469
Add validates and copies a skill directory, including supported bounded references.

96
00:07:37,469 --> 00:07:43,094
Installed narrows discovery to project skills, and force explicitly replaces an existing entry.

97
00:07:43,094 --> 00:07:49,385
The shipped workflows cover areas such as debugging, planning, verification, security, and interface work.

98
00:07:49,385 --> 00:07:53,760
They remain instructions for the agent, rather than a second execution engine.

99
00:07:55,558 --> 00:08:02,956
Impact follows reverse dependencies to show affected files and a deterministic risk score; breaking change comparison is incomplete.

100
00:08:02,906 --> 00:08:06,594
Trace accepts a stack trace, file, or standard input.

101
00:08:06,594 --> 00:08:10,406
Verify checks cited paths and can run configured commands.

102
00:08:10,406 --> 00:08:15,521
In our demo, symbol verification falsely rejected authenticate even though search found it.

103
00:08:15,521 --> 00:08:19,271
Refresh baseline is also not wired into the CLI action.

104
00:08:21,017 --> 00:08:27,560
Inspect HTTP probes a running local service with bounded output; remote targets require an explicit flag.

105
00:08:27,510 --> 00:08:32,490
Doctor checks runtime, index, agents, registrations, and provider sanity.

106
00:08:32,490 --> 00:08:37,896
Evaluate aggregates available evidence and configuration, not a complete live quality audit.

107
00:08:37,896 --> 00:08:40,323
Warden status checks availability.

108
00:08:40,323 --> 00:08:46,042
Warden run explicitly executes a command through an existing policy; the sandboxing tool is optional.

109
00:08:47,808 --> 00:08:54,915
CodeAtlas has a working context engine, toolkit, and MCP integration, with thirteen hundred ninety four tests passing.

110
00:08:54,865 --> 00:09:03,187
A broad beta still needs fixes: secret path filtering, implicit update checks, session recovery, visible agent output, and symbol verification.

111
00:09:03,187 --> 00:09:07,698
The production dependency audit also reports advisories requiring triage.

112
00:09:07,698 --> 00:09:12,479
Passing tests are valuable evidence, but they do not make these launch blockers disappear.

113
00:09:14,225 --> 00:09:16,331
Start with a small project.

114
00:09:16,281 --> 00:09:22,854
Index it, search a known symbol, ask for a bounded slice, and review an exported package before sending it to your agent.

115
00:09:22,854 --> 00:09:26,271
Use help on any command for every subcommand and flag.

116
00:09:26,271 --> 00:09:33,875
The companion includes the complete generated reference, real command captures, the beta audit, and editable Remotion source for this walkthrough.
